{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q17",
  "speechText": "One example was a production issue with large PDF processing jobs. During OCR, the memory usage kept increasing, and eventually the worker crashed. The main reason is that I did not want to assume the worker itself was the real problem. I wanted to find the root cause first. For example, I traced the processing flow, added targeted logging, checked the memory usage at important steps, and tested different batch sizes. From that, I found that the main bottleneck was the OCR CPU inference. Because of that, I first made the current flow more stable by using smaller batches and adding checkpoint and recovery logic, so if one step failed, we did not need to restart the whole job. At the same time, I handled the backend and workflow side and worked with DevOps to move the heavy OCR inference to a remote GPU service. So overall, I did not only focus on stopping the crash. I also checked worker stability, memory usage, job completion, and processing time to make sure the new solution was stable, observable, and safe to roll out.",
